INT. PET GROOMING SALON - AFTERNOON

Fluorescent lights. A wall of leashes. The air smells of oatmeal shampoo and panic.

CARMELITA VEGA (40s, rubber apron, forearms like a boxer's) lathers a very large, very wet Newfoundland named BISCUIT in a steel tub. Behind her, LEO PARK (20s, trainee, ear gauges) sprays the nozzle without looking. And in the waiting area, MR. ZELINSKI (70s, sweater vest, a tiny terrier under his arm) watches with deep concern.

CARMELITA
Leo, the nozzle.

LEO
Sorry, I was looking at the dog.

CARMELITA
The nozzle goes on the dog.

LEO
That's what I'm doing.

CARMELITA
You're spraying me.

Leo glances at the nozzle, then at Carmelita's drenched apron. He drops it. It whips around, spraying the ceiling.

CARMELITA (CONT'D)
Leo!

Both lunge for the hose. Biscuit shakes. A wave of soapy water crosses the salon. Mr. Zelinski clutches his terrier and rises from the chair.

Leo slips on the tile. Carmelita catches his collar. They freeze, mid-fall, staring at each other. Biscuit shakes again. Foam coats the window.

MR. ZELINSKI
Is this normal?

Carmelita and Leo exchange a look. Slowly, they straighten up. Leo wipes his face with his sleeve. Carmelita pulls a clump of suds from her hair.

CARMELITA
(flatly)
Every Tuesday.

LEO
Every Tuesday.

MR. ZELINSKI
I'll come back later.

He backs out the door. The bell jingles. Biscuit sneezes. Soap bubbles drift across the room, floating gently toward the ceiling.
